 ## Overview
-MaskGaussian Gradient Computation is a backpropagation technique used during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting models that incorporate probabilistic masks [S1]. It computes the gradient of the loss $\mathcal{L}$ with respect to the mask values $\mathcal{M}_i$ for each Gaussian $i$ contributing to a pixel [S1]. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting which optimizes only Gaussian parameters, this method explicitly differentiates through the mask-modulated alpha blending process to allow the masks to be learned or refined [S1]. It sets itself apart by decomposing the pixel color into a Gaussian-contributed part and a background-contributed part, ensuring the gradient accounts for how the mask affects both the visibility of the Gaussian and the transmission of the background [S1].
+MaskGaussian Gradient Computation is a method for computing the gradient of a loss function $\mathcal{L}$ with respect to per-Gaussian probabilistic masks $\mathcal{M}_i$ during the training of 3D Gaussian Splatting. It solves the problem of optimizing mask parameters that modulate Gaussian opacity, enabling adaptive Gaussian representation and pruning. It differs from standard 3DGS backpropagation by explicitly accounting for the interaction between the mask-modulated Gaussian contribution and the background color contribution, which is modulated by the final transmittance.
 
 ## How it works
+The method computes the gradient $\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial \mathcal{M}_i}$ for each Gaussian $i$ in the sorted list of $N$ Gaussians contributing to a pixel $\mathbf{x}$.
 
-### Inputs and Outputs
